On launch set angle 0, omega=spinRate.

Perfect hold still tracks airRot (braking rotations count slightly). Spin count = floor(airRot/TAU).

On landing, `off = angle mod TAU` minimized. Note if holding, angle near multiple of TAU → perfect.

Grading:
```
if off<0.30: grade perfect, power 1, dmg 0
else if off<0.72: grade one, power .74, dmg 11
else if off<1.35: grade body, power .52, dmg 22
else: grade head, power .34, dmg 34
```
But "arms" ~ side. Fine.

Bounce:
```
const staminaFactor = 1 - 0.035*(stats.stamina-1);
dmg = grade.dmg * staminaFactor; // with max integrity also scaled? That doubles. Use only max integrity scaling.
bounce = BOUNCE_BASE(jump) * grade.power * (1 + Math.min(spins,5)*0.075)
vx = grade.perfect ? Math.min(760, Math.max(vx*1.03, 330) + 60 + spins*22) :
     Math.min(760, Math.max(200, vx*0.72 + 40 + spins*8))
```
Hmm perfect vx growth: starting 300, each perfect +~80 → 500 after 3; cap 760. Good. Bad drops.

If impact < 180, no damage but still bounce small. Actually landing after a tiny hop with bad angle shouldn't amputate. Scale damage by impact factor `min(1, impact/420)`. And power maybe. Use `impactFactor = clamp(impact/420, 0.45, 1)`. damage *= impactFactor. Good.

Height: each frame compute `above = (terrain.rawHeightAt(x) - (y+radius)) / PX_PER_M` while airborne; maxHeight = max. But with camera/terrain descent, apex above local ground. Use raw ground below x. Since robot is in air, ground height at same x. Fine. For gaps use virtual height.

Distance: (x - startX)/8 clamp 0.

Chips reached mid-race:
- spins: when spins>=target (in a single jump) → chipSpins=true.
- height: when maxHeight >= target → chipHeight=true.
- distance: when distance >= distTarget → chipDist=true.
- first determined at finish.

UI flash when a chip achieved: popup + particles + sfx. Add to achieved set once.

Finish: x >= finishX. If player headMode, head crosses finish. Set finished. stop input. phase='finishing', finishTimer=0, confetti, rank computed. After 1.6s callback.

If destroyed: phase 'finishing' with result, after 2s callback.

Ranking: AI finish times recorded. On finish, rank = 1 + ais.filter(finished && finishTime <= playerFinishTime).length (AI finishing same frame?). Fine.

What if player destroyed after AIs finish: rank = total (DNF). Show destroyed.

Camera:
- targetX = focusX - 150 + clamp(vx*0.10, 0, 60) -? focus at 35% screen. view 480. robot at 160. target cam.x = x - 160.
- targetY = y - 330.
- smoothing lerp with `1 - exp(-6*dt)`.
- shake: add random offsets. Camera stores shake; decays.
- During intro: target robot at center x=240? cam.x = player.x-240, y = player.y-400; lerp faster.

Render:
```
ctx.save();
ctx.translate(shakeX, shakeY);
drawSky(ctx, theme, cam, time)
drawParallax
ctx.save(); ctx.translate(-cam.x, -cam.y); draw finish; draw terrain; draw debris; drawAIs; drawPlayer; drawParticles(world); popups; ctx.restore();
drawHUD
if intro drawBoot in world or screen coords
ctx.restore();
```
Careful: terrain decorations.

Finish banner drawing: at finishX, checkered vertical pole/banner from ground to ground-260. Because camera y follows, it may be offscreen until near. Draw flags. Also finish "line" on ground.

Player draw with rotation: ctx.translate(x,y); ctx.rotate(angle); drawRobot; restore. Add motion trails: draw 3 ghost silhouettes at previous angles with alpha if spinning fast. Maybe simple trail arcs. Add speed lines when vx>500.

Detached limbs drawn in world.

AI draw at theirs. Use drawRobot with angle; their heads attached. Add bounce shadow ellipse on terrain beneath? Nice: draw shadow ellipse at ground for all racers. Alpha based on height. Helps depth. Draw before sprites.

HUD: functions in race.ts or hud.ts. Separate hud.ts for cleanliness.

HUD needs race state: distance, rank, chips, spin gauge, level name. Could expose `Race.getHudState()` or draw directly. I'll put drawHUD(race) in hud.ts importing Race type? Circular. Instead hud.ts exports function with explicit args. Or keep in race.ts. I'll keep in race.ts but split helper. Fine.

Race class also handles touch hint DOM? Better App handles? Simpler: main triggers `race.held` from pointer state; touch hint is DOM shown at race start and hidden after 4s. Manage in main.

Pause: App.paused; draw pause overlay DOM. Race continues? update skipped.

Result generation must claim chips in save and unlock. That's App's job after callback (to keep Race pure-ish). Race returns achieved flags. App handles save and UI.

Question: chips achieved but race ends; claim all achieved. Award count = sum of newly claimed. For first chip rank must be 1 and finished. Compute `chipFirst` in result.

For multi mode, no chips.

Now UI rendering: big. Let me define `UI` class in ui.ts:

```ts
export class UI {
  root: HTMLElement;
  app: App;
  screen: Screen = 'title';
  render(screen, params)
}
```

To avoid circular import, UI receives callbacks object:
```ts
interface UIActions {
  startTournament(id?:number): void;
  startNextTournament(): void;
  startQuick(level:number, ai:number): void;
  startMulti(level:number, names:string[]): void;
  open(screen:Screen): void;
  upgrade(stat:keyof Stats): void;
  buySkin(i:number): void;
  setSetting(k,v): void;
  resetProgress(): void;
  resume(): void; restart(): void; quit(): void;
  pause(): void;
  audioUnlock(): void;
  nextRace(): void; replay(): void; toMenu(): void;
}
```
App implements. UI holds save ref via getter.

Simpler: UI takes App-like interface defined in ui.ts with methods and save. App implements interface. Circular type import of App would break runtime? Type-only import is fine. Use `import type { App } from './main'` maybe. But UI needs data. I'll define an interface `GameBridge` in ui.ts.

Implementation: `showScreen(name)` sets innerHTML, binds via root event listeners delegation (one listener installed once). `data-action` attributes with `data-value`.

Screens:
- title: logo, subtitle, menu buttons (Tournament, Quick Race, Pass & Play, Upgrades, How to Play, Settings). Show chips badge & robot skin preview maybe. Version footer. "Insert courage" joke.
- tournament: panel with header back + chips; scroll list of level cards. Each card: number badge, name, theme name, distance, difficulty pips, chips dots (4), lock icon. Click unlocked → app.startTournament(id). Also "Continue" button if any unlocked uncleared. Show "cleared" check.
- quick: list unlocked levels in a compact grid; each opens quick setup? Better: select level (cards) then options footer: AI count segmented 0/1/2/3 and Start button. Use local UI state (ui.quickLevel, quickAi).
- multi: select level, number of players 2/3, name inputs, "Start Pass & Play". After each heat, intermission overlay.
- upgrades: header; chips; four stat rows each with icon, name, level pips (10), effect, upgrade button with cost; robot preview swatch; skins grid; back.
- howto: instructions with diagrams (CSS/emoji? use inline SVG icons or text). Steps: kick, spin, hold to brake feet down, release for power, limbs fly, head last chance. Chip goals. Buttons back + play.
- settings: toggles music/sfx/screen shake; reset progress confirm modal; back.
- results: overlay panel with title (FINISHED / WRECKED), rank medal, stats grid, chips earned list (4 rows, new/earned/missed), total chips, buttons. Also "NEW LEVEL UNLOCKED".
- intermission (multi): "Player 2, get ready", stats of previous heat, Start button.
- multiResults: rankings + rematch/menu.
- pause: resume/restart/quit + sound toggles.
- howto etc.

Menus must be scrollable within frame. CSS: #ui absolute inset 0, pointer-events:none by default; screens with panels pointer-events:auto. For menus, overlay background gradient semi-transparent to show attract scene? Title should show canvas; other screens use a solid-ish blurred dark overlay.

Title logo with text: "KAMIKAZE" (big, tilted) and "ROBOTS" with badge. Use @fontsource/lilita-one. CSS text-shadow offset for chunky look; gradients via background-clip. Add little robot illustration? Canvas behind shows robot. Title logo position top.

If the canvas attract scene robot is at lower part, title buttons on top. Great.

Race HUD is canvas; DOM race-controls only pause/sound buttons at top-right? But canvas HUD has rank top-right. Position pause button bottom-right? Layout: top-right small pause/mute circles at (right 10, top 10) overlapping HUD? HUD has rank near right; place pause at top-right outside panel? Frame width 480; HUD panel margin 10 width 460; rank at right inside panel. Put pause/mute at bottom corners? Better: DOM buttons at top-right but HUD panel shortened to width 400? Hmm.

Alternative: draw pause icon in canvas at top-right (x=444,y=30) and handle click region; but DOM buttons easier. Let's design HUD panel width 460 and include a pause button slot at far right bottom? Put DOM controls at bottom-right, small circular buttons above spin gauge? Could clutter.

Use DOM race-controls fixed at top-right *outside* the HUD? Since HUD is inside frame, buttons would overlap. Let's make HUD top bar height 84, and place pause/mute buttons at bottom-left (small circles), away from spin gauge bottom-right. Bottom-left has "HOLD TO BRAKE" hint though. Move hint above. Or top-left: inside HUD left edge? I'd rather draw pause in canvas HUD: a small circle at right edge of panel row 1, with "II" glyph, and mute. Handling clicks: add pointerdown listener to canvas, check if within pause button rect before setting held. On intro too. That's clean and scales. For sound toggle, put in pause overlay/settings only. Pause button in HUD: top-right at x=436,y=36 r=18. Then rank placement left of it.

Actually simpler: HUD panel top row: distance left, rank center-right, pause button far right. Use click hit region. Add muted indicator? skip; settings accessible in pause.

Pause overlay has Resume, Restart, Quit, plus Music

